It seems that I misunderstood your request. Here is a complete Java program that simulates a simple database and provides a `printTable` method to print its contents in a tabular format:

```java
public class Database {
    private String[][] data;

    public Database() {
        this.data = new String[0][0];
    }

    public void addRow(String[] values) {
        if (data.length == 0 || values.length != data[0].length + 1) {
            System.out.println("Invalid row size. Expected " + (data.length * data[0].length));
            return;
        }
        String[] newValues = new String[data.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            newValues[i] = values[i];
        }
        System.arraycopy(data, 0, newValues, 0, data[0].length);
        newValues[newValues.length - 1] = values[values.length - 1];
        data = new String[data.length + 1][data[0].length + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            System.arraycopy(newValues, 0, data[i], 0, data[i].length);
        }
    }

    public void printTable() {
        if (data.length == 0) {
            return;
        }
        String[] columns = {"Name", "Age"};
        for (int i = 0; i < data[0].length; i++) {
            System.out.print(columns[i] + ": ");
        }
        System.out.println();
        for (String[] row : data) {
            for (int j = 0; j < row.length - 1; j++) {
                System.out.print(row[j] + " | ");
            }
            System.out.println(row[row.length - 1]);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Database db = new Database();
        
        // Create some sample data
        String[][] data1 = {{"John", "25"}, {"Anna", "30"}};
        String[][] data2 = {{"Peter", "35"}, {"Linda", "40"}};

        System.out.println("Data 1:");
        db.addRow(data1);
        db.printTable();
        
        System.out.println("\n\nData 2:");
        db.addRow(data2);

        // Create more sample data
        String[][] data3 = {{"Tom", "50"}, {"David", "60"}};
        String[][] data4 = {{"James", "75"}, {"William", "85"}};

        System.out.println("Data 3:");
        db.addRow(data3);
        db.printTable();
        
        System.out.println("\n\nData 4:");
        db.addRow(data4);

        // Add a row with an empty string to test the addRow method
        String[] values = new String[0];
        db.addRow(values);
    }
}
```

This program creates a `Database` class that can store data in a table format. It uses two nested arrays to represent rows and columns, respectively. The `printTable` method prints the contents of the database to the console.

In the `main` method, some sample data is created, added to the database using the `addRow` method, and then printed out using the `printTable` method. Additionally, a row with an empty string is added to test the `addRow` method.
